{{also|DINK}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/dɪŋk/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ɪŋk|lang=en}}

===Etymology 1===
Imitative. Originally {{w|United States|US}}. Attested since the 1930s.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|tennis}} A soft [[drop shot]].

=====Translations=====
{{trans-top|sports}}
* Dutch: {{t+|nl|lobben}}
{{trans-mid}}
{{trans-bottom}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|tennis}} To play a soft drop shot.
# {{lb|en|football}} To [[chip]] lightly, to play a light [[chip]] shot.
#: ''The forward '''dinked''' the ball over the goalkeeper to score his first goal of the season.''
#* {{quote-journal
|date=December 28, 2010
|author=Kevin Darlin
|title=West Brom 1 - 3 Blackburn
|work=BBC
|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/eng_prem/9308862.stm
|page=
|passage=But the visitors started the game in stunning fashion when Morten Gamst Pedersen '''dinked''' forward a clever looping pass and Kalinic beat the offside trap, surged into the box and beautifully placed the ball past goalkeeper Scott Carson.}}

===Etymology 2===
Origin unknown. Attested since the 1930s.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|Australia|colloquial}} A ride on the crossbar or handlebars of a bicycle.
#: ''I gave him a '''dink''' on my bike.''

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|Australia|colloquial}} To carry someone on a pushbike: behind, on the crossbar or on the handlebar.
#* '''1947''', [[w:John Lehmann|John Lehmann]] (editor), ''The Penguin New Writing'', Issue 30, [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=z9EzAAAAMAAJ&q=bicycle+%22dink%22&dq=bicycle+%22dink%22&hl=en&ei=ereATbXSB82qcbWd2P4G&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=66&ved=0COECEOgBMEE page 103],
#*: I didn't like them at all ; only the lame one who used to let me '''dink''' him home on his bicycle.

===Etymology 3===
Origin unknown. Attested since the 1960s. Compare {{m|en|Chink}}, a derogatory term for a Chinese person.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|US|military|_|slang|pejorative|dated}} A North Vietnamese [[soldier]].
#* {{quote-book|1989|Craig Roberts and Charles W. Sasser|The Walking Dead: A Marine's Story of Vietnam||197|Our job was to go out on night patrols and stay behind to zap any '''dinks''' we caught sneaking back to their holes at dawn.}}

===Etymology 4===
Initialism. Originally US. Attested since the 1980s.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|US}} Double Income No Kids - a childless couple with two jobs.

===Etymology 5===
See {{m|en|dinkum}}.

====Adjective====
{{en-adj|?}}

# {{lb|en|Australia|New Zealand}} Honest, fair, true.
# {{lb|en|Australia|New Zealand}} Genuine, proper, [[fair dinkum]].

====Adverb====
{{en-adv|-}}

# {{lb|en|Australia|New Zealand}} Honestly, [[truly]].
#* {{quote-book|2006|Pip Wilson|Face in the Street||323|Are you The Banjo? Fair '''dink''' no bull? Oh, sorry, lady, I mean ... dinkidi?}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun|-}}

# {{lb|en|Australia|Northern England}} Hard work, especially one's share of a task.
# {{lb|en|historical|dated}} A soldier from Australia or New Zealand, a member of the [[ANZAC]] forces during the First World War.

===Etymology 6===
Origin unknown. Attested since the late nineteenth century.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|North America|colloquial|slang}} A penis.
#* {{quote-book|2004|Brian Francis|Fruit: A Novel about a Boy and his Nipples||2|The hair on my legs is softer than the hair around my '''dink''', but it still grosses me out.}}
# {{lb|en|North America|colloquial|slang}} A foolish person, a despised person. {{defdate|from 1960s}}
#* {{quote-book|1997|Chris Gudgeon|You’re Not as Good as You Think||13|{{...}}he was a '''dink''', and all the money, fame, and power in the world wouldn't change that one simple fact.}}

===Etymology 7===
Origin unknown. Attested in English and in {{w|Scots language|Scots}} since the sixteenth century.

====Adjective====
{{en-adj|-}}

# {{lb|en|archaic}} Finely dressed, elegant.
#* {{quote-book|1821|Walter Scott|Kenilworth||249|All these floated along with the immense tide of population, whom mere curiosity had drawn together; and where the mechanic in his leathern apron, elbowed the '''dink''' and dainty dame, his city mistress{{...}}}}

===Etymology 8===
See {{m|en|dinq}}.

